      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the value(s) of \\( x \\) for which \\( f(x) = f^{-1}(x) \\), we first need to determine the inverse function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) of \\( f(x) = 3x - 8 \\).\n\nLet's start by setting \\( y = f(x) \\):\n\\[ y = 3x - 8 \\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = 3x - 8 \\]\n\\[ y + 8 = 3x \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{y + 8}{3} \\]\n\nThus, the inverse function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) is:\n\\[ f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x + 8}{3} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the value(s) of \\( x \\) for which \\( f(x) = f^{-1}(x) \\). This gives us the equation:\n\\[ f(x) = f^{-1}(x) \\]\n\\[ 3x - 8 = \\frac{x + 8}{3} \\]\n\nTo eliminate the fraction, multiply both sides of the equation by 3:\n\\[ 3(3x - 8) = x + 8 \\]\n\\[ 9x - 24 = x + 8 \\]\n\nNext, isolate \\( x \\) by subtracting \\( x \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 9x - x - 24 = 8 \\]\n\\[ 8x - 24 = 8 \\]\n\nThen, add 24 to both sides:\n\\[ 8x = 32 \\]\n\nFinally, divide both sides by 8:\n\\[ x = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) for which \\( f(x) = f^{-1}(x) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{4} \\]",
